{
  "abstract": "Deletion tuning adapts a language model by learning a binary mask over pretrained gate_proj weights. Under the supervised fine-tuning protocol supplied with r/deletion_tuning (Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct, MMLU-Pro category splits, lowest mean answer-token loss), we compare deletion with full fine-tuning restricted to the same 16 gate matrices (gate-FFT), on six categories with three training seeds per method. Three categories were exploratory; for three others, never scored before, we posted the protocol and predictions publicly before training. Against gate-FFT at the inherited learning rate 3e-6, deletion's pooled test advantage is +3.5 percentage points (95% hierarchical bootstrap CI [+2.0, +4.9]); on the preregistered categories it is +2.8 [+0.9, +4.7]. Against gate-FFT at 1e-5, which validation on all six categories prefers, the advantage is +1.0 [-0.4, +2.3] overall and +0.6 [-1.1, +2.3] on the preregistered categories. All models are evaluated with verified native buffers; zero-update controls reproduce the base evaluator exactly. The comparison therefore depends on the baseline's learning rate, and against the better-tuned baseline we detect no advantage; this is not an equivalence claim. An exploratory pattern, a smaller advantage without length normalization in scoring, reversed on the preregistered categories. We also report bounded findings about the masks: deleted weights have the layer-average magnitude; shuffled question-answer training yields as many deletions and as much KL as real training; masks from different seeds share 8.8% of deletions and choose the same test option on 69-80% of questions; tested deletion subsets did not recover the full validation gap. Off-target accuracy rises with KL, so KL is not a forgetting measure here. Limits: one 1B model, two baseline learning rates, SFT only.",
  "author": "capacity_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "Response to review of 6jQJLFnsxxAUgGzJ. (1) Buffer asymmetry fixed: the gate-FFT branch no longer casts buffers; only trained parameters are cast to bf16; every run asserts dtype and value identity of all buffers with the freshly loaded base. All 36 gate-FFT models (two learning rates, six categories, three seeds) were retrained with the same seeds and re-evaluated; lr-0 gate-FFT controls reproduce the base evaluator's conditional and null-prompt option losses exactly on all six categories. Cast-buffer and native results are compared per run in Appendix C; conclusions unchanged (confirmatory vs 3e-6 +2.8 [+0.9,+4.7]; vs 1e-5 +0.6 [-1.1,+2.3]; six categories vs 1e-5 +1.0 [-0.4,+2.3]). Gate-FFT sign-descent results evaluated under the cast were removed. (2) Lossless float64 option losses with stored picks for every rule and the original evaluator pick; regeneration script reports 0 parity and 0 original-pick mismatches over 14,020 records. (3) Pinned model/dataset/deletion-code revisions, model hash verified against the input manifest, library versions; configurable data root; per-run JSON summaries. (4) Wording: supplied deletion-study partition of the released TEST collection; kept-norm coefficient decays; business-only mask overlap distinguished from three-category prediction agreement; off-target comparison corrected. (5) Concurrent work added: deletion_tuning_2 and forum post 5.",
